# C#/.NET testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic testing rules to xUnit/NUnit
convention and disciplined mocking with Moq/NSubstitute. Applies only to
`*.cs` files.

## Framework and layout

- Match whichever test framework the project already uses (xUnit or
  NUnit are both common; do not introduce a second one into a project
  that has standardized on the other). xUnit: `[Fact]` for a single
  case, `[Theory]` + `[InlineData(...)]`/`[MemberData(...)]` for
  parameterized cases. NUnit: `[Test]` and `[TestCase(...)]` play the
  same roles.
- Tests live in a mirrored `*.Tests`/`*.Test` project (the standard .NET
  convention: `Orders/` production code, `Orders.Tests/` its tests), not
  inside the production project itself.
- Name a test method for behavior, not implementation:
  `MethodName_Scenario_ExpectedResult`
  (`Validate_MissingOrderId_ThrowsArgumentException`) reads as a
  specification; `Test1`/`ValidateTest` does not.

## Table-driven (`Theory`/`TestCase`) tests

- Use `[Theory]` + `[InlineData(...)]` (xUnit) or `[TestCase(...)]`
  (NUnit) for a set of cases that exercise the same method with
  different inputs/expected outputs, instead of copy-pasting a near-
  identical `[Fact]`/`[Test]` per case — each data row becomes its own
  reported test result, so a failure names exactly which input failed.
- Reach for `[MemberData(nameof(Cases))]`/a `TestCaseSource` when a case
  needs a non-primitive value (an object, a collection) that attribute
  arguments cannot express directly.

## Async tests

- A test that exercises `async` production code is itself `async Task`
  (`public async Task Validate_ValidOrder_ReturnsSuccess()`), and awaits
  the call under test — never `async void` (the test runner cannot await
  it, and any assertion failure or exception inside it is lost instead
  of failing the test) and never a blocking `.Result`/`.Wait()` on the
  Task under test as a substitute for `await`.

## Mocking boundaries: mock the seam, not the internals

- Mock/stub an **external dependency the unit under test talks to
  through an interface** — an `HttpClient`-backed API client behind an
  interface, a repository interface wrapping the database, a clock/time
  provider, a message publisher — using Moq (`Mock<IThing>`) or
  NSubstitute (`Substitute.For<IThing>()`).
- Do **not** mock an internal collaborator that is itself part of what
  the test is supposed to verify — a private helper method, a concrete
  value object, a piece of the same class's own logic split into another
  method for readability. Mocking one layer too deep proves only that
  the mock returns what you told it to, not that the unit under test
  actually behaves correctly; it also makes the test brittle to harmless
  internal refactors that change nothing about observable behavior.
- The question to ask before adding a mock: "does the real system cross
  a process/network/disk boundary here (HTTP, database, filesystem,
  clock, random), or am I mocking a class my own code owns and could
  just construct for real?" Only the former earns a mock.
- Verify a mock's interaction (`mock.Verify(...)`/
  `receivedCall.Received()`) only for a call whose occurrence is itself
  part of the contract being tested (e.g. "an email is sent exactly
  once") — do not add interaction verification for calls that are
  incidental implementation detail, which locks the test to today's
  internal wiring.

## Assertions and determinism

- Use the project's already-adopted assertion style (xUnit's built-in
  `Assert`, or FluentAssertions if already a dependency) — do not
  introduce a second assertion library into a project that has
  standardized on one.
- Never synchronize with `Thread.Sleep`/`Task.Delay` to wait for an
  async operation or background work to finish — await the actual
  `Task`, or use a real synchronization primitive
  (`TaskCompletionSource`, a cancellation-bounded wait) so the test is
  deterministic and not flaky under load.
- Reset/dispose any shared test fixture (`IClassFixture<T>`, a
  `WebApplicationFactory<TEntryPoint>` for integration tests) between
  cases that mutate shared state, so one test's leftovers cannot change
  another's result depending on run order.
